(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the cure rate of catheter ablation for atrioventricular nodal reentry?","choices":["About 50%","About 70%","About 90%","Over 95%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation offers a select subset of patients with atrioventricular nodal reentry the opportunity for a definitive cure, in over 95 percent. Most episodes are otherwise well tolerated and almost always end with vagal maneuvers or adenosine. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does the rhythm of multifocal atrial tachycardia compare with focal atrial tachycardia?","choices":["Regular; focal is irregular","Both are regular","Both lack P waves","Irregular; focal is regular"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Focal atrial tachycardia is regular because a single ectopic focus drives it, while multifocal atrial tachycardia is irregular, with at least three distinct P-wave shapes in the same lead from multiple foci. Both keep one P wave for every QRS, with a constant PR of 0.12 to 0.20 seconds. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How do most people with premature junctional contractions present?","choices":["With syncope","With chest pain","With a stroke","Without symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most people with premature junctional contractions are not symptomatic. No treatment is needed if the beats are asymptomatic and infrequent, and reassurance of the benign nature of the rhythm is often enough; a beta blocker is kept for persistent symptoms or frequent beats. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How common is hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["The usual presentation","Tied to a wide QRS complex","Mainly in older adults","Less common than stable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachycardia is the less common presentation. Many patients are asymptomatic or have transient symptoms if it is paroxysmal, and supraventricular tachycardia usually does not lead to syncope or hemodynamic collapse unless the heart rate goes above 200. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 26"},{"q":"Which is a physiologic cause of sinus tachycardia?","choices":["Pain or anxiety","Hypovolemia","Pulmonary embolism","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sinus tachycardia is classed by cause, and pain and anxiety are the listed physiologic causes, along with exercise and conditions that raise catecholamine release. Physiologic sinus tachycardia usually comes without symptoms such as dizziness or nausea, and it settles when the cause is treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Sinus tachycardia is defined by a sinus rate in which range?","choices":["From 60 to 100","Above 100, up to 160","Above 160","From 250 to 300"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sinus tachycardia is a regular sinus rhythm above 100 up to 160, with a uniform P wave for every QRS, a PR of 0.12 to 0.20 seconds and a narrow QRS. Above 160, a regular narrow tachycardia with hidden P waves is described as supraventricular tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When is catheter ablation used for premature atrial contractions?","choices":["Whenever any are found","When they cause no symptoms","When caffeine triggers them","When they trigger fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation is used when premature atrial contractions are frequent or are triggering atrial fibrillation. Otherwise, persistent symptomatic beats get a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated, and asymptomatic ones get no treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"In hemodynamically stable atrial fibrillation, what is the first aim of management?","choices":["A permanent pacemaker","Rate control","Immediate rhythm conversion","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In hemodynamically stable atrial fibrillation the first aim is rate control, given intravenously if the patient is in rapid ventricular response, with anticoagulation planned for all. Rhythm options such as outpatient cardioversion after 21 days of anticoagulation, or mapping and ablation, come later. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which trigger can precipitate atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia?","choices":["Increased vagal tone","Caffeine","Hypothyroidism","Beta blockers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is commonly seen without heart disease and may occur spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine, alcohol, beta-agonists or sympathomimetics. Caffeine is therefore a listed trigger, and patients typically report sudden palpitations with neck pulsations (frog sign). — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Besides athletes and sleepers, which adult group commonly shows sinus bradycardia?","choices":["Adults over 65","Adults with anxiety","Adults with hyperthyroidism","Adults using stimulants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sinus bradycardia can be a normal variant, seen as an adaptive response in well-conditioned or active people, during sleep, and in adults over age 65. Most people with it are asymptomatic, and asymptomatic sinus bradycardia is not treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram pattern defines sinus arrhythmia?","choices":["Irregularly irregular with no P waves","Regular with sawtooth baseline waves","Irregular with three P-wave shapes","Cyclic irregularity, normal P waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sinus arrhythmia is a normal, physiologic rhythm that is irregular with a pattern, faster with inspiration and slower with expiration, at 60 to 100. A uniform P wave precedes every narrow QRS with a PR of 0.12 to 0.20 seconds, so only the spacing of the beats changes. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia?","choices":["Amiodarone","A permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia is treated first with atropine. If it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows; chronic symptomatic bradycardia is managed with a permanent pacemaker, and asymptomatic bradycardia is not treated at all. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which drug is used for persistent symptoms from premature junctional contractions?","choices":["Metoprolol","Adenosine","Atropine","Procainamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If symptoms from premature junctional contractions persist, a beta blocker such as metoprolol is used if not contraindicated; frequent beats, over 10 per minute or multifocal, are also treated with a beta blocker after a search for heart disease. Infrequent, asymptomatic beats need only reassurance. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"After how much anticoagulation can stable atrial fibrillation be electively cardioverted as an outpatient?","choices":["24 hours","21 days","3 months","1 year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elective outpatient cardioversion of atrial fibrillation follows 21 days of anticoagulation, the same interval used before elective cardioversion of atrial flutter. Anticoagulation is part of management for all patients with atrial fibrillation, guided by the stroke-risk and bleeding-risk scores. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia depends on which structure?","choices":["Three atrial foci","A damaged sinus node","An accessory pathway","A circuit inside the node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is seen in patients with an accessory pathway, such as in Wolff-Parkinson-White, and the reentry circuit uses that pathway alongside the atrioventricular node. Pre-excitation on a sinus tracing, a short PR and delta wave, signals the pathway, and ablating it is first-line long-term treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the definitive management of atrial flutter?","choices":["Vagal maneuvers","Ablation","Adenosine","A pacemaker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ablation is the definitive management of atrial flutter. Flutter has a substantial recurrence rate and is harder to rate control than atrial fibrillation, so although stable patients start with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker, ablation is what ends the problem. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which cardiac event is a listed cause of junctional rhythm?","choices":["Hypovolemic shock","Untreated hyperthyroidism","Inferior myocardial infarction","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause of junctional rhythm, alongside sinus node dysfunction such as sick sinus syndrome, significant bradycardia, the acute period after cardiac surgery and digoxin toxicity. Junctional rhythm can also appear in healthy athletes with high vagal tone and is generally well tolerated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"In sick sinus syndrome, a fast run that is irregularly irregular with no P waves alternates with pauses. Which rhythm is the fast run?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fast run that is irregularly irregular with no P waves is atrial fibrillation. In tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which a tachyarrhythmia alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or pauses, atrial fibrillation is the most common cause of the fast rhythm. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"When premature junctional contractions are frequent (over 10 per minute), what is done?","choices":["Check for heart disease","Reassurance alone","Anticoagulation","A permanent pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Premature junctional contractions that are frequent, over 10 per minute or multifocal, prompt a look for underlying structural heart disease, and the beats are treated with a beta blocker. By contrast, infrequent beats without symptoms need no treatment beyond reassurance. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"In sinus arrest, which rhythm may take over?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","A junctional rhythm","Atrial flutter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In sinus arrest the sinus node does not generate an impulse, which leads to a junctional rhythm taking over, typically at 40 to 60 with inverted or hidden P waves. This differs from sinus exit block, in which the impulse is generated but cannot get out of the node. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Atrial flutter is commonly seen in which patients?","choices":["Raised intracranial pressure","Scarring after heart surgery","Young women using caffeine","Healthy, conditioned athletes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrial flutter is commonly seen in patients with atrial scarring or damage, for example after surgery, as well as with right-sided heart failure or pulmonary vascular disease, and in patients with atrial fibrillation who are taking an antiarrhythmic. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which is a recognized cause of sick sinus syndrome?","choices":["Heavy caffeine intake","Surgical injury to the node","Excess thyroid hormone","Anemia from blood loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Listed causes of sick sinus syndrome include sinoatrial node ischemia, injury to the sinoatrial node from cardiac surgery, electrolyte abnormalities, medications such as beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin, and hypothyroidism. Management starts with treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the management of asymptomatic sinus bradycardia?","choices":["No treatment","Atropine","A temporary pacemaker","A permanent pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sinus bradycardia can be a normal variant, and most people with it are asymptomatic; no treatment is given if there are no symptoms. Atropine, then a temporary pacemaker, is for acute, symptomatic, unstable bradycardia, and a permanent pacemaker for chronic symptomatic disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does a premature junctional contraction look like?","choices":["Early narrow QRS, P inverted or hidden","Early wide QRS without a P wave","Early, abnormal upright P wave","Sawtooth waves before the QRS"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A premature junctional contraction begins within the atrioventricular junction and gives an early, narrow QRS, usually 0.10 seconds or less, with an inverted P wave, a P wave buried in the QRS (appearing as no P wave) or a P wave after the QRS. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which type of atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is most common?","choices":["Orthodromic","Antidromic","Slow-fast","Fast-slow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow-fast, or typical, atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is the most common type of nodal reentry. It is a regular, narrow complex tachycardia, usually 140 to 180, with hidden P waves from a reentry circuit within the atrioventricular node. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How do patients with atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia most commonly present?","choices":["Palpitations","Stroke","Heart failure","Bradycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia is seen in patients with an accessory pathway, such as Wolff-Parkinson-White, and patients most commonly present with palpitations. The rhythm is regular, usually 200 to 300, with hidden or retrograde P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"In a stable narrow complex tachycardia, which step comes before any drug?","choices":["A vagal maneuver","An echocardiogram","Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In stable supraventricular tachycardia, the narrow complex tachycardias, a vagal maneuver is first line, before any drug. Adenosine follows if needed, may be repeated once, and may convert the rhythm or slow it enough to show P waves; synchronized cardioversion is kept for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is a delta wave?","choices":["An inverted T wave","A sawtooth baseline","A slurred upstroke of the QRS","A notched, wide P wave"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A delta wave is a slurred upstroke at the start of the QRS complex. With a short PR interval under 0.12 seconds and a wide QRS, it marks pre-excitation from an accessory pathway in Wolff-Parkinson-White. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which pair are both listed risk factors for atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Young age and female sex","Low weight and anemia","Hypertension and diabetes","Hypothyroidism and hypotension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension and diabetes are both listed; atrial fibrillation is commonly seen in patients with hypertension, diabetes, cardiac disease, a family history of atrial fibrillation, obesity, sleep apnea, excessive alcohol and older age. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with some rates approaching 300 appears in someone with a known accessory pathway. Which intravenous drug is used?","choices":["Adenosine","Atropine","Magnesium","Procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregular, wide-complex tachycardia with some rates approaching 300 in someone with a known accessory pathway is atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. When Wolff-Parkinson-White is known, intravenous procainamide is used, and adenosine must never be given. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
